{#
    The product field every admin screen shares. Include it rather than copying it.

    It lives in core because core is the only layer no bundle can delete: `config/bundles.php`
    discovers modules with glob(), and `bundle_status` switches them off, so a partial owned by one
    bundle and included by another is a 500 waiting for the day somebody removes the first. The
    reasoning in full is on App\Service\Product\ProductPicker.

    Parameters
    ----------
    field         required  the input name, e.g. 'lines[0][product_id]' or 'product_id'
    products      required  the options to carry — ProductPicker::options(), or any rows carrying
                            the keys `optionData` and `optionText` name
    remote        required  ProductPicker::isRemote(): past the inline limit, search takes over
    selected      optional  the currently-chosen ProductCore, or null
    selectedId    optional  the chosen product's id, for a caller holding an id rather than an
                            entity — a sell-side line row knows `line.productId` and nothing more.
                            Beats `selected` when both are given; falsy (0, '', null) means none.
    form          optional  a form id, when the control sits outside its <form> (#613)
    required      optional  defaults to false
    label         optional  aria-label; defaults to 'Product'. `false` renders no aria-label at all,
                            which is what a control already labelled by its surroundings wants.
    placeholder   optional  overrides the placeholder, for a field that may legitimately be blank
    searchUrl     optional  where tier 2 fetches matches; defaults to core's plain product search.
                            A purchase screen passes its own, because it prices the results against
                            a vendor's rate card and core knows nothing about vendors.
    selectClass   optional  extra classes on the <select>, for a screen whose JavaScript binds to a
                            hook of its own. Appended; the widget's own classes are not negotiable.
    optionData    optional  the `data-*` payload each <option> carries, as {suffix: key} — the
                            attribute name WITHOUT its `data-` prefix, mapped to the key read off
                            each product row. Defaults to `{sku: 'sku'}`, which is what a screen
                            that only has to name the product needs. A key a given row does not
                            carry renders as the empty string rather than raising: one payload can
                            then serve documents whose rows differ by a key (the order's carry
                            `tracksBatch`; the quote's and the invoice's do not), which is the whole
                            point of a payload the caller states once.
    optionText    optional  the keys whose values make up an option's visible text, joined with
                            ' — '. Defaults to `['sku', 'name']`.

    ## Why the payload and the option text are parameters and not a branch

    The ten inventory and purchasing screens that call this — nineteen call sites between them —
    want one `data-sku` and a SKU-and-name label, because all they do with the answer is save it.
    The sell side's three want eight attributes and a bare name, because its line JavaScript fills
    the rest of the row — weight, unit, tax code, cost, original price, price, batch tracking — off
    the chosen option without another round trip, and the SKU already has a column of its own one
    cell along.
    That is a difference in what the CALLER does with the answer, so it is an argument. It must
    never become `{% if document == 'order' %}`: the moment this file knows which screen it is
    rendering it is a dozen templates in one and worse than the copies it replaced.

    The failure mode the shapes exist for is silent. A missed `data-*` throws nothing, logs nothing
    and renders a select that looks perfect; the row simply stops filling in. So the defaults are
    today's behaviour exactly, and each attribute is asserted individually, by name and value, off
    a rendered page — see SellSideProductFieldCest.

    The three tiers are #399's, from the sell side's order form.
#}
{% set fieldName = field %}
{% set manualName = field ends with ']' ? field[:-1] ~ '_manual]' : field ~ '_manual' %}
{% set pickerSearchUrl = searchUrl is defined and searchUrl ? searchUrl : path('admin_product_picker_search') %}
{% set pickerIsRequired = required|default(false) %}
{% set pickerLabel = label is defined ? label : 'Product' %}
{% set pickerClass = ['js-searchable-select', remote ? 'js-only' : '', selectClass|default('')]|filter(v => v)|join(' ') %}
{% set pickerSelectedId = selectedId|default(null) ?: (selected is defined and selected ? selected.id : null) %}
{% set pickerOptionData = optionData|default({'sku': 'sku'}) %}
{% set pickerOptionText = optionText|default(['sku', 'name']) %}
{# Tier 1 has no server round-trip to ask for a barcode match, so each option carries its own
   product's codes as search data instead — see App\Twig\ProductBarcodeSearchExtension and
   app.js's filter(), which already reads data-sku the same way. #}
{% set pickerBarcodeData = product_barcode_search_data(products) %}

{# js-searchable-select is the shared widget; it upgrades this select in place when scripting is
   on. With scripting off the select is still a real select, which a browser makes type-to-search
   for free — which is the whole reason tier 1 needs no fallback at all. #}
<select name="{{ fieldName }}"
        class="{{ pickerClass }}"
        {% if form is defined and form %}form="{{ form }}"{% endif %}
        data-placeholder="{{ placeholder is defined and placeholder ? placeholder : (remote ? 'Type to search products…' : 'Select a product…') }}"
        {% if remote %}data-search-url="{{ pickerSearchUrl }}"{% endif %}
        {# `required` goes on the control the admin can actually reach. Past the inline limit this
           select is `js-only`, i.e. display:none with scripting off — and a browser refuses to
           submit a form carrying a required control it cannot focus to complain about (core's own
           order form records the same trap at templates/admin/order/form.html.twig). So past the
           limit the requirement moves to the <noscript> box below, which is the visible control in
           that tier. The server refuses a missing product either way; that is what backs it. #}
        {% if pickerIsRequired and not remote %}required{% endif %}
        {% if pickerLabel is not same as(false) %}aria-label="{{ pickerLabel }}"{% endif %}>
    <option value=""></option>
    {% for product in products %}
        <option value="{{ product.id }}"
{# The loop tags sit in column 0 so the payload keeps the attribute-per-line layout this field has
   always rendered, byte for byte: Twig drops the newline that immediately follows a `%}`, so a
   tag indented with its body would silently pull each attribute onto the line above. #}
{% for dataName, dataKey in pickerOptionData %}                data-{{ dataName }}="{{ attribute(product, dataKey) is defined ? attribute(product, dataKey) : '' }}"
{% endfor %}                data-barcodes="{{ pickerBarcodeData[product.id] is defined ? pickerBarcodeData[product.id] : '' }}"
                {{ pickerSelectedId and pickerSelectedId == product.id ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ pickerOptionText|map(key => attribute(product, key))|join(' — ') }}</option>
    {% endfor %}
</select>

{#
    Past the inline limit the select above carries only what this screen already names, so with
    scripting off there is no option for anything else and no way to name a product at all — the
    search endpoint is reachable only from JS. This is the no-JS way in.

    <noscript> and not a hidden input or a CSS class, deliberately: a hidden input still POSTS, so
    with scripting on it would post alongside the select and the two would fight over which wins.
    Inside <noscript> a scripting-enabled parser treats the whole thing as text, so it is not in the
    DOM at all and the widget never sees it.

    Its name is `*_manual` rather than a second copy of the field name, so nothing depends on which
    of the two PHP happens to parse last. Every controller reads the select first and falls back to
    this, through one function — ProductPicker::idFromPostedRow().

    The derivation above understands one convention, the indexed one: `lines[0][product_id]` gives
    `lines[0][product_id_manual]`, and a bare `product_id` gives `product_id_manual`. Every caller
    in the application posts one of those two shapes — the sell side's three reconciled on
    `lines[N][field]` before this field was adopted — so no caller overrides it and there is no
    parameter to do so. Write the override rather than making this line cleverer if one ever does.
#}
{% if remote %}
    <noscript>
        <label class="no-js-product-id">
            Product ID
            <input type="number" min="1" step="1" name="{{ manualName }}"
                   value="{{ pickerSelectedId ?: '' }}"
                   {% if form is defined and form %}form="{{ form }}"{% endif %}
                   {% if pickerIsRequired %}required{% endif %}>
        </label>
        <small>Find a product's ID at <code>{{ pickerSearchUrl }}{{ '?' in pickerSearchUrl ? '&' : '?' }}q=</code> (2+ characters).</small>
    </noscript>
{% endif %}
